{"name":"Hay Wain","hex":"#cdad59","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hay wain","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large open vehicle, drawn by horses, used to carry loads of hay; a haywagon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hay_wain"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Hay Wain","blocks":[{"text":"The Hay Wain – originally titled Landscape: Noon – is a painting by John Constable, completed in 1821, which depicts a rural scene on the River Stour between the English counties of Suffolk and Essex. It hangs in the National Gallery in London and is regarded as \"Constable's most famous image\" and one of the greatest and most popular English paintings. It is considered as one of the quintessential paintings from the Romantic movement of arts and literature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/John_Constable_-_The_Hay_Wain_%281821%29.jpg/330px-John_Constable_-_The_Hay_Wain_%281821%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Hay_Wain","wikidata":"Q2366825"},{"title":"Hay","blocks":[{"text":"Hay is grass, legumes, or other herbaceous plants that have been cut and dried to be stored for use as animal fodder, either for large grazing animals raised as livestock, such as cattle, horses, goats, and sheep, or for smaller domesticated animals such as rabbits and guinea pigs. Pigs can eat hay, but they do not digest it as efficiently as herbivores do."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Roundbale1.jpg/330px-Roundbale1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hay","wikidata":"Q336989"},{"title":"Wagon","blocks":[{"text":"A wagon is a heavy four-wheeled vehicle pulled by draft animals or on occasion by humans, used for transporting goods, commodities, agricultural materials, supplies and sometimes people."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Wagon_Ride_P9190071.jpg/330px-Wagon_Ride_P9190071.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagon","wikidata":"Q859281"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Flatford_Mill%2C_site_of_the_HayWain.JPG/500px-Flatford_Mill%2C_site_of_the_HayWain.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Flatford_Mill%2C_site_of_the_HayWain.JPG/1280px-Flatford_Mill%2C_site_of_the_HayWain.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Flatford_Mill%2C_site_of_the_HayWain.JPG/500px-Flatford_Mill%2C_site_of_the_HayWain.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The site in Flatford, Suffolk where The Hay Wain was painted, now a tourist destination, in 2010.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlatford_Mill%2C_site_of_the_HayWain.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Hayfield2.jpg/500px-Hayfield2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Hayfield2.jpg/500px-Hayfield2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHayfield2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Bales_from_corn_stems_01.jpg/500px-Bales_from_corn_stems_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Bales_from_corn_stems_01.jpg/1280px-Bales_from_corn_stems_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Bales_from_co